{
	title: 'Enter steers between rounds; Ctrl-Enter interrupts',
	spec: "In the terminal and the web, while a turn runs, plain Enter sends the prompt as a between-rounds steer: it joins the inbox without aborting the model's stream or a running tool, and the turn delivers it before its next request (or continues after a finished answer to deliver it). Ctrl-Enter keeps the interrupting steer: the stream and running tools stop (unsafe-to-stop calls still finish first, task ker) and the turn goes on with the message. The web Steer button interrupts. Alt-Enter still queues after the turn. On an idle, paused or failed session, Enter and Ctrl-Enter behave alike. The submit command carries nextRound: true for the between-rounds kind; without it a submit interrupts, as older clients expect. The help rows and key help name both keys. In a terminal, Ctrl-Enter needs modified-Enter reporting (kitty keyboard protocol, e.g. Ghostty); elsewhere it arrives as plain Enter.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'The flag is only sent while the session is busy, so idle submits stay unchanged. A nextRound steer after Escape still swaps the controller so the stopped turn goes on, as an interrupting steer does. While a turn runs, the help row drops shift-enter: with ctrl-enter added, it truncated esc: pause at 80 columns. Uploads waiting to finish resume as plain Enter, so a Ctrl-Enter sent during an upload becomes a between-rounds steer.'
	],
	needs: ['2r', 'ker'],
	foldInto: ['2r']
}
